      "latency_ms": 5444.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 765,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is a vital component of life and exists in three primary states: **liquid** (common at room temperature), **solid** (ice, when frozen), and **gas** (water vapor, when heated). \n\n### Key Properties:\n1. **Polarity**: Water molecules are polar, meaning they have a slight positive charge on one end (hydrogen) and a negative charge on the other (oxygen). This makes water an excellent **solvent**, capable of dissolving many substances, which is crucial for biological processes.\n2. **High Specific Heat**: Water absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments (e.g., stabilizing Earth’s climate and body temperature).\n3. **Cohesion & Adhesion**: Water molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling phenomena like capillary action in plants and the formation of water droplets.\n4. **Unique Expansion Upon Freezing**: Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water. This allows ice to float, insulating aquatic life in cold climates.\n\n### Role in Nature and Life:\n- **Water Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, and precipitation, sustaining ecosystems and weather patterns.\n- **Biological Function**: It acts as a medium for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients, and maintains cell structure in all living organisms.\n- **Abundance**: Found in oceans, rivers, lakes, groundwater, and even within living cells (e.g., 60% of the human body is water).\n\nWater’s unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and countless natural and industrial processes."
